A developer, a reviewer, and a security lead must not merge a pull request; only a maintainer may do so.

For a pull request targeting an ordinary branch such as "main," a maintainer may merge it only if it has been formally approved, no matter how many approvals it has collected.

Unless a pull request has collected enough approvals to cross the required approval-count threshold, it may not be merged without that formal approval.

Once a pull request reaches the required approval-count threshold, for example five approvals, a maintainer may merge it even though it has not received a formal approval mark.

This threshold exception only applies when the target branch's name matches or begins with a protected release prefix such as "release/."

A pull request with five approvals targeting "release/2.4" may therefore be merged without a formal approval, since it meets the threshold on a qualifying release branch.

A request by a maintainer to merge a pull request targeting "release/3.0" that has crossed the approval threshold, with no other prohibition in play, must be able to succeed.

