## Access-Control Requirement
The system manages the authorization of electrical grid switching orders before field execution.

The people involved are planners, shift supervisors, operators, control room operators, and auditors. One person may hold both the planner and the shift supervisor role at the same time, and one person may hold both the operator and the control room operator role at the same time.

The resources are switching orders and lockout tags. Every switching order records the planner who created it, the operator who executes it, its feeder, its voltage level, its circuit count, its status, its scheduled start, and its scheduled end. Every lockout tag records the order it belongs to, who applied it, and its feeder.

Each request carries the current time, whether it is made from the SCADA control network, whether lockout-tagout has been verified, and whether an outage emergency has been declared.

A planner or a shift supervisor may authorize a switching order.

A switching order may be authorized only while it is still in the planned state, and an order that has left the planned state may not normally be authorized.

That planned-state requirement is relaxed when the request falls within the order's scheduled switching window, between its scheduled start and scheduled end, so within that window an order may be authorized even if it is no longer in the planned state, while outside the window the planned-state requirement stands.

The planned-state requirement is also relaxed when the order's voltage level is above the threshold that requires shift-supervisor authorization, so such a high-voltage order may be authorized by a shift supervisor even when it is not in the planned state, while at or below that voltage level the planned-state requirement stands.

Neither a planner nor a shift supervisor may authorize an order that has left the planned state, unless one of the relaxations above applies.

A request by a planner to authorize a switching order that trips none of the prohibitions above must be able to succeed.

