## Access-Control Requirement
The system manages access to pages in an internal company wiki.

The people involved are contributors, reviewers, space admins, and readers. One person may hold both the contributor and reviewer role at the same time.

The resources are pages and spaces. Every page records its author, reviewer, space, classification, external-sharing setting, labels, and status.

Each requester carries their clearance and the spaces they belong to.

Each request carries whether it is on the corporate network, the current time, and whether emergency access has been declared.

People use the system to view pages, edit pages, review pages, and enable external sharing.

A reader, contributor, reviewer, or space admin may view a page only when their clearance is high enough for the page's classification.

When a page has external sharing enabled, a reader, contributor, reviewer, or space admin may view it even if their clearance is not high enough for the page's classification.

When emergency access has been declared, a space admin may view a page even if its classification is above the space admin's clearance.

When emergency access has not been declared and external sharing is not enabled, a space admin must meet the page's classification before viewing it.

A reader, contributor, reviewer, or space admin must not view a page whose classification is above their clearance unless external sharing is enabled or the requester is a space admin using declared emergency access.

When emergency access has been declared, the space admin's emergency access wins over the normal clearance requirement.

A request by a reader to view a page that does not trip any prohibition above must be able to succeed.

