## Access-Control Requirement
The system manages the prescribing, verification, and refill authorization of controlled-substance medications at an outpatient pharmacy.
The people involved are pharmacy technicians, staff pharmacists, pharmacists in charge, and prescribers. One person may hold both the staff pharmacist and the pharmacist in charge role at the same time.
The resources are prescriptions, which record the prescriber, the patient, who entered the prescription, who verified it, the prescriber's DEA registration number, the controlled-substance schedule level, the refills remaining, and the status.
Dispense records track which prescription was filled, who dispensed it, the quantity dispensed, and the copay amount charged.
Each request carries whether the prescription drug monitoring program was checked, whether multi-factor authentication was used, whether the requester is connected to the pharmacy network, the current time, and whether an emergency override is in effect.
Only a pharmacist in charge may authorize a refill on a prescription.
A pharmacist in charge may authorize a refill only if that same pharmacist is the one who originally verified the prescription.
When a different pharmacist verified the prescription, the pharmacist in charge handling the refill request must not authorize it.
A pharmacist in charge may authorize a refill only when the prescriber's DEA registration number follows the standard format of two letters followed by seven digits with a valid checksum digit.
When the prescriber's DEA registration number does not follow that format, the refill must not be authorized, even by the pharmacist who verified the prescription.
A pharmacy technician, a staff pharmacist who is not also serving as pharmacist in charge, and a prescriber must not authorize a refill.
A request by a pharmacist in charge who verified the prescription themselves, where the prescriber's DEA registration number is properly formatted, to authorize the refill must be able to succeed.

