The people involved are pharmacy technicians, staff pharmacists, the pharmacist in charge, and prescribers. One person may hold both the staff pharmacist and pharmacist in charge roles at the same time.

The resources are prescriptions and dispense records. Every prescription records its prescriber, patient, entered-by person, verified-by pharmacist, prescriber's DEA number, schedule level, refills remaining, and status.

Every dispense record records its prescription, the person who dispensed it, quantity, and copay amount.

Each requester carries dispense authority and a state license.

Each request carries whether the PDMP was checked, whether MFA was used, whether the request comes from the pharmacy network, the current time, and whether an emergency override is being used.

When the copay amount crosses the money threshold and the request comes from the pharmacy network, a prescription may be dispensed only after it has been verified.

When the copay amount does not cross the money threshold, the verified-first requirement does not apply.

When the request does not come from the pharmacy network, the verified-first requirement does not apply.

Neither a staff pharmacist nor the pharmacist in charge may dispense medication for an unverified prescription when the copay amount crosses the money threshold and the request comes from the pharmacy network.

A request by a staff pharmacist to dispense a medication that does not trip any prohibition above must be able to succeed.

