The people involved are recruiters, hiring managers, interviewers, and coordinators. One person may hold both the recruiter and interviewer roles at the same time, and one person may hold both the recruiter and hiring manager roles at the same time.

The resources are applications, interview feedback, and offers. Every application records its candidate, who referred the candidate, the hiring team, the requisition code, the sensitivity of the candidate's personal information, and its status.

Every interview feedback record records the application it relates to, the interviewer who wrote it, the candidate, and its status.

Every offer records the application it belongs to, who extended it, the compensation amount, the signing bonus, and its status.

Each requester carries a personal-information clearance level, and each request carries whether it comes from the corporate network, the current time, and whether executive approval has been granted.

A hiring manager may approve an offer only while it is still in drafted status.

A hiring manager must not approve an offer once it has moved past drafted status.

A hiring manager must not approve an offer whose compensation crosses the executive-approval tier of $250,000, unless the request carries executive approval.

For offers with compensation of $250,000 or less, a drafted offer may be approved without executive approval.

A hiring manager must not approve an offer whose total compensation, base pay plus signing bonus, crosses $300,000, unless the request carries executive approval.

For offers whose total compensation is $300,000 or less, a drafted offer may be approved without executive approval.

A request by a hiring manager to approve a drafted offer that trips none of these compensation thresholds must be able to succeed.

