The people involved are pickers, forklift operators, zone supervisors, warehouse managers, and security officers. One person may hold both the picker and the forklift operator role at the same time.

The resources are orders and zones. Every order records who requested it, its picker, its items, its item count, its pick-list code, its status, its destination zone, and when it was released. Every zone records its zone code, its sensitivity, its zone group, whether it is locked, and its operational status.

Each request carries the current time, whether the requester has badged in on-site, and whether an emergency has been declared.

In this workflow an order is first picked and then its destination zone is accessed, and the zone-access step can see the order's destination zone, its status, and its picker as recorded by the picking step.

A picker or a forklift operator may pick an order only after it has been released, unless the picker holds the forklift-operator role required for palletized picks, in which case the order may be picked as part of that palletized handling.

When an order has not been released and the picker does not hold the forklift-operator role for palletized picks, the order may not be picked.

A picker, a forklift operator, or a zone supervisor may access a zone only when their badge clearance level covers the zone's sensitivity level, and an associate whose clearance does not reach that sensitivity may not enter.

Neither a picker, a forklift operator, nor a zone supervisor may access a zone whose sensitivity exceeds their badge clearance level.

A run through the workflow by a picker who picks a released order and then accesses its destination zone with clearance covering the sensitivity, tripping no prohibition above, must be able to succeed.

