## Access-Control Requirement
The system manages the authorization of outgoing bank wire transfers.
The people involved are originators, verifiers, treasury officers, and compliance reviewers. One person may hold both the originator and the verifier role at the same time.
The resources are wire transfers and accounts. Every wire transfer records who initiated it, who verified it, who screened its beneficiary, the account it draws from, its amount, fee, amount tier, beneficiary, currency code, whether it is flagged high risk, and its status.
Every account records its holder and the beneficiaries it is allowed to send funds to.
Each request carries the current time, the bank's daily processing cutoff, whether it originates on the secure banking network, whether multi-factor authentication was used, and whether an emergency has been declared.
An originator may initiate a wire transfer.
A verifier or treasury officer who initiated a wire transfer may not approve it.
A verifier or treasury officer may approve a wire transfer only when the request is on the secure banking network.
When the request is not on the secure banking network, a verifier or treasury officer may not approve the wire transfer, even if they did not initiate it.
A treasury officer may release an approved wire transfer for payment.
A compliance reviewer may screen a beneficiary before a wire transfer is approved.
A request by a verifier, made on the secure banking network, to approve a wire transfer they did not initiate must be able to succeed.

